{"causes":["The fragmentation of the Roman Empire during the Crisis of the Third Century created a need to document its rulers and their actions.","The rise of political instability and usurpation prompted the authors to capture the tumultuous history of the time.","The cultural legacy of earlier Roman historians inspired the Scriptores Historiae Augustae to compile imperial biographies."],"effects":["The Augustan History influenced the way subsequent generations viewed Roman emperors, blending historical fact with narrative embellishment.","It contributed to the development of historiography, showcasing the challenges of recording history in a politically charged environment.","The text has served as a primary source for modern historians, shaping contemporary understandings of Roman imperial authority and governance."],"relationships":[{"sourceSlug":"scriptores-historiae-augustae","sourceName":"Scriptores Historiae Augustae","verb":"CREATES","targetSlug":"augustan-history","targetName":"Augustan History","context":"The Scriptores Historiae Augustae compiled the Augustan History as a collection of biographies focusing on Roman emperors from 117 to 284 AD."},{"sourceSlug":"roman-empire","sourceName":"Roman Empire","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"augustan-history","targetName":"Augustan History","context":"The political and social structures of the Roman Empire directly influenced the narratives found within the Augustan History."},{"sourceSlug":"crisis-of-the-third-century","sourceName":"Crisis of the Third Century","verb":"OCCURS_IN","targetSlug":"augustan-history","targetName":"Augustan History","context":"The Augustan History documents events occurring during the Crisis of the Third Century, a pivotal period in Roman history."},{"sourceSlug":"hadrian","sourceName":"Hadrian","verb":"CONTAINS","targetSlug":"augustan-history","targetName":"Augustan History","context":"The biography of Hadrian is one of the prominent accounts featured in the Augustan History, reflecting his reign from 117 to 138 AD."},{"sourceSlug":"severan-dynasty","sourceName":"Severan Dynasty","verb":"FRAMES","targetSlug":"augustan-history","targetName":"Augustan History","context":"The accounts of the Severan Dynasty within the Augustan History frame the narrative of imperial power and succession during the early 3rd century."}],"places":[{"name":"Rome, Italy","role":"Cultural center"},{"name":"Trier, Germany","role":"Birthplace"},{"name":"Antioch, Turkey","role":"Major historical site"}],"texts":[{"title":"Wikipedia: Augustan History","type":"Reference article","slug":"augustan-history"}],"externalLinks":["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q722678","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Historia_Augusta"],"tags":["literary work","Histories & Chronicles","cause-and-effect"],"thumbnailUrl":"","quote":"","legacySummary":"Augustan History contributed to the historical record of the Classical period. Key lasting impacts include: Contributed to cultural heritage and knowledge transmission. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.","summary":"The Historia Augusta (Latin: Scriptores Historiae Augustae, 'Writers of Augustan History') is a collection of biographies of Roman emperors from Hadrian (r. 117–138 CE) to Numerian and Carinus (d. 285 CE), purportedly written by six different authors in the reigns of Diocletian and Constantine (c. 295–330 CE), but almost certainly the work of a single forger writing c. 360–400 CE who invented most of the six 'authors', fabricated documents, letters, and quotations, and interpolated vast amounts of fictional material into what appears to be a continuation of Suetonius's Lives of the Twelve Caesars. The Historia Augusta is simultaneously the most controversial text in Latin historical literature — a documented forgery in which fiction is interwoven with genuine historical tradition in ways that remain impossible to disentangle — and an indispensable primary source for the history of the 2nd and 3rd century Roman Empire, particularly for the difficult period of the Crisis of the Third Century (235–284 CE) for which it is sometimes the only surviving narrative source.

The Historia Augusta's historical unreliability has been known since the 17th century, and modern scholarship (particularly the work of Hermann Dessau in 1889, who definitively demonstrated its 4th-century forgery character) has established that many of its supposed documents, quoted letters, and biographical details are invented. Yet for the period 235–284 CE — for which Cassius Dio, Herodian, and other historians either do not survive or break off — the Historia Augusta is sometimes the only narrative source, however unreliable. Its biographies of Gordian III, Philip the Arab, Decius, Valerian, and the 'soldier emperors' of the Crisis period are our primary (and deeply problematic) narrative accounts of one of the most turbulent periods in Roman imperial history.

The mystery of who wrote the Historia Augusta — what purpose the elaborate fiction of multiple authorship served, and why a 4th-century author would fabricate biographies of 2nd and 3rd century emperors — remains one of the great puzzles of ancient literary history, and the work continues to generate more scholarly controversy than almost any other Latin text.","subjects":["Roman History","Classical Era","Late Antiquity","Latin Literature","Historical Forgery","Roman Empire","Source Criticism","Biography"],"frameworks":["STRUCTURAL_ANALYSIS","CAUSE_AND_EFFECT"],"historicalSignificance":{"significanceScore":7,"significanceNarrative":"The Augustan History shaped the understanding of Roman emperors and governance, influencing historical narratives and interpretations for generations. Its blend of fact and fiction has made it a critical source for studying the complexities of imperial authority during a time of crisis.","significanceCategory":"regional"},"_editLog":[]}
